WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:04.304
-I have a question for you.
Can I ask you?

00:00:04.304 --> 00:00:06.072
-Yeah, please.
-Okay, cool.

00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:12.679
These are just things
I've been curious about you.

00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:17.383
Who do you wish you'd been
closer to that you weren't?

00:00:17.383 --> 00:00:18.785
-In my life?

00:00:18.785 --> 00:00:21.688
-Um, it'd be nice if it
overlapped with people I know.

00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:23.523
[ Laughter ]

00:00:23.523 --> 00:00:25.091
-You know what I mean?
Just for my own interest.

00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:26.892
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-If you start talking

00:00:26.892 --> 00:00:28.428
about someone
from New Hampshire,

00:00:28.428 --> 00:00:30.930
other than Ghislaine Maxwell...
-I wish I was, like, closer

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:33.633
with, like, my grandmother,
but you don't want that.

00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:35.668
-You do?
-Well, my brother was closer

00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:36.736
with her.
-Why?

00:00:36.736 --> 00:00:39.038
-Because when she,
like, got old,

00:00:39.038 --> 00:00:42.442
she moved to San Diego and he
would, like, drive to see her

00:00:42.442 --> 00:00:44.710
all the time.
-And you wouldn't?

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:46.712
-Well, I didn't live
in California.

00:00:46.712 --> 00:00:48.414
-Oh, well, then, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:00:48.414 --> 00:00:50.149
But I will say, I think,
if I lived in California,

00:00:50.149 --> 00:00:51.918
I wouldn't have made the drive.
-What would they do?

00:00:51.918 --> 00:00:53.420
Yeah, that's
what I was thinking.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:55.788
-But I think it makes me feel
like, "Oh, he's just nicer."

00:00:55.788 --> 00:00:57.824
-Is he?
I mean, but what did they do?

00:00:57.824 --> 00:00:59.725
Hang out?
-Yeah, he would go down there.

00:00:59.725 --> 00:01:02.061
She lived with two of my aunts,
and they would hang out.

00:01:02.061 --> 00:01:04.664
-Huh. And did he like it,
or he felt he needed to do it?

00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:07.366
-No, I think he really liked it.
-He actually liked hanging

00:01:07.366 --> 00:01:08.601
with that crew?
-Yeah.

00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:10.803
I mean, my grandmother was
a great lady,

00:01:10.803 --> 00:01:12.171
but I just would -- Yeah.

00:01:12.171 --> 00:01:14.607
-Yeah, grandmas are great ladies
to their grandchildren.

00:01:14.607 --> 00:01:17.243
-How was your -- You
name-dropped your grandmother

00:01:17.243 --> 00:01:18.711
in the monologue.
-Yeah.

00:01:18.711 --> 00:01:20.713
-Was she happy
with the monologue?

00:01:20.713 --> 00:01:23.582
Have you heard back?
-She thought it was very funny.

00:01:23.582 --> 00:01:27.086
-You gave her, like -- You
made her have the punch line.

00:01:27.086 --> 00:01:30.823
-Oh, yeah, no, I mean,
she's -- she's, uh --

00:01:30.823 --> 00:01:34.694
she's got, like,
Joan Crawford-esque lines

00:01:34.694 --> 00:01:36.262
coming out of her mouth
constantly.

00:01:36.262 --> 00:01:38.397
-[ Laughs ]
-And everything about her --

00:01:38.397 --> 00:01:40.499
Like, my sense, if you're like,
"Where did you get

00:01:40.499 --> 00:01:42.735
your stand-up sensibilities?"
like, I'm doing my Nana.

00:01:42.735 --> 00:01:44.770
Uh...
[ Both laugh ]

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:46.873
So, yeah, no, she got
the last laugh.

00:01:46.873 --> 00:01:49.542
That was when she told me that
if she wasn't my grandmother,

00:01:49.542 --> 00:01:52.011
she wouldn't know who I was,
which I thought was great.

00:01:52.011 --> 00:01:54.580
-I was thinking about,
while you were talking,

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:56.582
'cause I wasn't really
tuned in...

00:01:56.582 --> 00:01:57.883
-Sure.
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:57.883 --> 00:02:01.721
Closer with -- somebody I wish
I was closer with --

00:02:01.721 --> 00:02:04.957
like, our friend Jost just
married Scarlett Johansson.

00:02:04.957 --> 00:02:06.925
-Yeah.
-We've talked about this.

00:02:06.925 --> 00:02:10.396
We didn't know you could, like,
talk to hosts.

00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:13.933
-No.
I knew you could talk to them.

00:02:13.933 --> 00:02:17.803
But the idea of, like, you know,
"May I have this dance?"

00:02:17.803 --> 00:02:19.438
[ Laughter ]
Like, are you out of your mind?

00:02:19.438 --> 00:02:22.242
Like, are you out of your mind?
[ Laughter ]

00:02:22.242 --> 00:02:24.777
But, you know, Jost also --

00:02:24.777 --> 00:02:27.012
we've written those musicals
together.

00:02:27.012 --> 00:02:31.250
And it's not divided up like
I'm music and he's insanity.

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:35.555
But, like, you know, like, the
sparks sprang out of the wall

00:02:35.555 --> 00:02:36.989
in the LaGuardia sketch

00:02:36.989 --> 00:02:38.825
that almost hit
the flying Jake Gyllenhaal.

00:02:38.825 --> 00:02:40.459
-Yeah.
-That's Jost's idea, you know.

00:02:40.459 --> 00:02:42.561
So that's the kind of confidence
that's makes you go up

00:02:42.561 --> 00:02:44.831
to a movie star and go like,
"Hey, what are you doing later?"

00:02:44.831 --> 00:02:46.499
[ Laughter ]

00:02:46.499 --> 00:02:50.202
Yeah, I think the closest I got
was I, uh --

00:02:50.202 --> 00:02:53.439
I asked if Danny DeVito
would come meet my wife.

00:02:53.439 --> 00:02:54.741
[ Laughter ]

00:02:54.741 --> 00:02:56.276
Which, you know, was awesome,
but...

00:02:56.276 --> 00:02:57.810
-At a party.
Like, he didn't have to

00:02:57.810 --> 00:02:59.712
leave a party or anything.
-No, he just had to

00:02:59.712 --> 00:03:01.647
get out of his booth,
which was awkward, though.

00:03:01.647 --> 00:03:03.850
You ever seen someone get out
of the middle of the booth

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:05.517
that's just like that
for, like, an hour.

00:03:05.517 --> 00:03:09.488
So, he got out and, yeah...
But, no, I never, um...

00:03:09.488 --> 00:03:12.825
I mean, all the people
I could have dated, you know?

00:03:12.825 --> 00:03:16.763
-I know, let it go 'cause
we were focused on the work.

00:03:16.763 --> 00:03:18.965
-Sigourney Weaver.
-Sigourney Weaver.

00:03:18.965 --> 00:03:20.433
-She hosted.
-Helen Mirren.

00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:22.401
I wish I was close
with Helen Mirren.

00:03:22.401 --> 00:03:24.904
-I had a box of chocolates and
flowers for Helen Mirren.

00:03:24.904 --> 00:03:26.973
And I was gonna ask her
to go to the Rainbow Room,

00:03:26.973 --> 00:03:28.507
and then I thought,
"You know what?

00:03:28.507 --> 00:03:30.710
There's probably
a 'no dating the host' policy."

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:32.779
[ Laughter ]
But he married her.

00:03:32.779 --> 00:03:35.181
They married each other.
-Yeah.

00:03:35.181 --> 00:03:37.650
-That's the way you say it now.
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:40.853
-And so it doesn't
break company policy.

00:03:40.853 --> 00:03:41.921
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:03:41.921 --> 00:03:44.223
-Good. You have
anything else to ask me?

00:03:44.223 --> 00:03:45.924
-Yeah, I got a bunch of stuff.
-Great.

00:03:45.924 --> 00:03:47.926
-How was Jost's wedding?
-It was really nice.

00:03:47.926 --> 00:03:49.762
-Yeah, I was really bummed
I wasn't invited.

00:03:49.762 --> 00:03:52.398
Do you think
you are good-looking?

00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:54.099
-Do I think I'm good-looking?
-Yeah.

00:03:54.099 --> 00:03:56.502
-I think I'm, uh --
I think I'm okay.

00:03:56.502 --> 00:03:58.804
But I'm not, like --
I think I'm fine.

00:03:58.804 --> 00:04:00.272
-Really?
-I don't look at --

00:04:00.272 --> 00:04:04.543
I will say, I don't ever, like,
look in the mirror

00:04:04.543 --> 00:04:07.280
and obsess over anything.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.482
But I'm not, like,
va-va-va-voom.

00:04:09.482 --> 00:04:10.816
-Oh, you're not, like,
va-va-va-voom.

00:04:10.816 --> 00:04:11.850
-Yeah.
-Okay.

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:13.485
Do you have
va-va-va-voom moments?

00:04:13.485 --> 00:04:16.522
-Um, you know what?
Here's the thing.

00:04:16.522 --> 00:04:20.293
I think I maybe think I'm
a little bit better-looking

00:04:20.293 --> 00:04:22.594
than I am when
I look at a photo.

00:04:22.594 --> 00:04:25.231
I'm sometimes like, "Oh,
I was a little off."

00:04:25.231 --> 00:04:27.032
-Yeah.
-Yeah. You know what I mean?

00:04:27.032 --> 00:04:28.567
So maybe I do think
I'm good-looking,

00:04:28.567 --> 00:04:30.369
but then photos bring me
back down to Earth.

00:04:30.369 --> 00:04:32.405
-Yeah, I often see photos
of myself onstage,

00:04:32.405 --> 00:04:34.240
and I go, "I wish
I could be that guy."

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:36.442
Uh, but...
-But I'm the opposite.

00:04:36.442 --> 00:04:39.011
I'm saying, I think,
in my head...

00:04:39.011 --> 00:04:40.913
-Okay.
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:40.913 --> 00:04:42.448
I think, in my head,
I'm like, "Alright,

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:44.183
I think I look good,"
and then I'll see a photo

00:04:44.183 --> 00:04:45.784
of myself onstage, and
I'm like, "Oh, boy."

00:04:45.784 --> 00:04:47.653
-Well, that's all angles, too...
-I got some bad angles.

00:04:47.653 --> 00:04:49.221
-...and some college newspaper
photographers,

00:04:49.221 --> 00:04:51.290
like, right under me,
and say, "Hey, buddy."

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:54.726
Again, 38. When I'm 25,
you can take those angles.

00:04:54.726 --> 00:04:58.097
Okay, next question.
Have I ever hurt your feelings?

00:05:02.568 --> 00:05:05.271
-I don't think so.
-Okay.

00:05:05.271 --> 00:05:06.905
-But I think there are times

00:05:06.905 --> 00:05:08.908
that I've worried
you were mad at me.

00:05:08.908 --> 00:05:10.676
-Oh.
-Yeah.

00:05:10.676 --> 00:05:12.812
-Oh, no.
-I think that maybe you don't --

00:05:12.812 --> 00:05:16.215
No, but I think this is just how
people at the show communicate.

00:05:16.215 --> 00:05:18.418
Like, I was always worried
that maybe,

00:05:18.418 --> 00:05:20.820
if I had to give you bad news
at the show,

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:22.588
that sometimes
you were mad at me

00:05:22.588 --> 00:05:24.723
and wouldn't say,
"I'm mad at you."

00:05:24.723 --> 00:05:26.525
-Mm, mm.

00:05:26.525 --> 00:05:30.996
Uh, I have heard that feedback
before from others.

00:05:30.996 --> 00:05:33.632
Uh, no, I was never mad at you.

00:05:33.632 --> 00:05:36.569
Uh, I was mad at the "system,"

00:05:36.569 --> 00:05:40.506
which was just a straw man
sometimes.

00:05:40.506 --> 00:05:44.777
Uh, but, no.
But I -- I know that I maybe

00:05:44.777 --> 00:05:48.981
didn't give off, like,
the correct, like,

00:05:48.981 --> 00:05:51.717
"Hey, I know you're just
giving me the news, buddy."

00:05:51.717 --> 00:05:53.685
-I think it's a lot
for me to ask, as well,

00:05:53.685 --> 00:05:55.021
as being the giver of bad news,

00:05:55.021 --> 00:05:56.756
that then the person
I'm giving it to

00:05:56.756 --> 00:05:58.357
has to make me feel okay
about it.

00:05:58.357 --> 00:06:00.292
So it's, like,
a little unfair to say.

00:06:00.292 --> 00:06:02.428
-It was often after we were
up 36 hours.

00:06:02.428 --> 00:06:03.963
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:06:03.963 --> 00:06:08.668
I think I often was disappointed
when something that, like,

00:06:08.668 --> 00:06:11.703
Simon Rich and Marika Sawyer
and I wasn't picked.

00:06:11.703 --> 00:06:14.072
I was disappointed there wasn't,
like, a revolution

00:06:14.072 --> 00:06:15.341
from the staff.
-Right.

00:06:15.341 --> 00:06:17.310
-I was like, "Well, surely,
there will be unrest

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:18.610
until it's put in."
You know?

00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:20.713
[ Laughter ]
But, no, I feel --

00:06:20.713 --> 00:06:22.515
-No, you shouldn't feel --
I feel --

00:06:22.515 --> 00:06:24.717
But I mostly -- I think it's
more the value is

00:06:24.717 --> 00:06:26.885
that you never --
you never hurt my feelings.

00:06:26.885 --> 00:06:28.821
-Oh, yeah.
You've never hurt my feelings.

00:06:28.821 --> 00:06:31.223
-I'm glad to hear that.
-Yeah, uh...

00:06:31.223 --> 00:06:33.526
you have a very high EQ.

00:06:33.526 --> 00:06:35.795
Emotional Q.
-Oh.

00:06:35.795 --> 00:06:38.030
-I don't know
what "IQ" stands for.

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:40.132
-[ Laughs ]
-Intelligence Quota?

00:06:40.132 --> 00:06:41.634
I don't know.
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:41.634 --> 00:06:43.869
-I was gonna take a --
My psychiatrist was like,

00:06:43.869 --> 00:06:46.738
"Do you want to take a formal
test to see if you have ADHD,

00:06:46.738 --> 00:06:48.841
which you definitely have?"
And I was like, "Sure."

00:06:48.841 --> 00:06:50.876
And it's a neuropsych test.
And she said,

00:06:50.876 --> 00:06:53.512
"You will find out your IQ
on this test.

00:06:53.512 --> 00:06:56.348
It just -- It's also listed.
So, if, psychologically,

00:06:56.348 --> 00:06:58.484
you don't think
you could handle that,

00:06:58.484 --> 00:07:00.453
maybe we should not
take the test."

00:07:00.453 --> 00:07:02.554
And I was like,
"If I have a low IQ,

00:07:02.554 --> 00:07:05.624
I'm gonna be thrilled, 'cause,
like, I beat the system."

00:07:05.624 --> 00:07:07.159
You know?
[ Laughter ]

00:07:07.159 --> 00:07:08.961
I got a coat. Look at me.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:08.961 --> 00:07:13.766
Um, uh, do you think
about your own funeral?

00:07:13.766 --> 00:07:17.169
-Um...no.

00:07:17.169 --> 00:07:18.637
-Really?
-Yeah.

00:07:18.637 --> 00:07:20.639
-Oh.
-Do you think about yours?

00:07:20.639 --> 00:07:23.242
-Yeah. Do other people?
-Yeah.

00:07:23.242 --> 00:07:25.344
-Yeah. Okay.

00:07:25.344 --> 00:07:28.047
-I think, sometimes --
I think -- You know what?

00:07:28.047 --> 00:07:30.682
I think, when I get close to it,
I try to stop myself.

00:07:30.682 --> 00:07:33.285
-When you almost start thinking
about your funeral?

00:07:33.285 --> 00:07:35.054
-Yeah.
-Like, you'll be driving,

00:07:35.054 --> 00:07:37.189
and you're like,
"I'm picturing flowers.

00:07:37.189 --> 00:07:38.924
That's a casket.
Calm down, Seth."

00:07:38.924 --> 00:07:41.227
-I should point, the only thing
I think about is

00:07:41.227 --> 00:07:43.729
what people will say.
-That's what I'm talking about.

00:07:43.729 --> 00:07:46.665
-I'm not talking about the
casket color or the pallbearers.

00:07:46.665 --> 00:07:48.534
[ Laughter ]
I'm not talking about

00:07:48.534 --> 00:07:50.836
which cast members
I make carry my dead body

00:07:50.836 --> 00:07:53.706
up a marble floor of a church.
[ Laughs ]

00:07:53.706 --> 00:07:57.543
I'm gonna make them carry
a long, long, long church.

00:07:57.543 --> 00:08:00.613
No, it's about the speeches.
-Do you think about it a lot?

00:08:00.613 --> 00:08:03.382
-Yes. I would like people
to include my faults.

00:08:03.382 --> 00:08:05.618
-Interesting.
-And not be like,

00:08:05.618 --> 00:08:09.454
"He was always there for you."
It's, like, I wasn't always.

00:08:09.454 --> 00:08:11.890
You know, I tried to do --
I'm not dead,

00:08:11.890 --> 00:08:14.026
but I'm gonna speak
in past tense.

00:08:14.026 --> 00:08:16.395
I tried to do the best I could.

00:08:16.395 --> 00:08:19.632
And sometimes I didn't try
to do the best I could.

00:08:19.632 --> 00:08:23.168
But I -- my intention
was always kindness.

00:08:23.168 --> 00:08:25.671
But I wouldn't mind
if someone was like,

00:08:25.671 --> 00:08:27.740
"He was late a lot.
He was late.

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:30.910
And we talked to him about it,
and he was still late."

00:08:30.910 --> 00:08:33.412
[ Laughter ]
And he would,

00:08:33.412 --> 00:08:36.782
even into his late 30s,
would say it was the traffic.

00:08:36.782 --> 00:08:39.951
And it was that he left late."
Or, "He --"

00:08:39.951 --> 00:08:43.089
-Boy, that is a tough thing
to ask somebody to say,

00:08:43.089 --> 00:08:45.658
'cause it's gonna be...
-I would like --

00:08:45.658 --> 00:08:48.627
I just want it said to everyone.
Like, "Listen,

00:08:48.627 --> 00:08:51.030
don't sugarcoat it."

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:53.198
Like, can you imagine
being at my funeral,

00:08:53.198 --> 00:08:55.067
and they just said
positive things,

00:08:55.067 --> 00:08:57.269
and they weren't like,
"He kind of was like

00:08:57.269 --> 00:08:59.105
a little rotten prince
sometimes."

00:08:59.105 --> 00:09:00.673
[ Laughter ]

00:09:00.673 --> 00:09:01.974
Like, you thought
I was mad at you

00:09:01.974 --> 00:09:03.476
when you were just telling me
my sketch wasn't --

00:09:03.476 --> 00:09:06.044
-Now I'm worried
that you're gonna think

00:09:06.044 --> 00:09:08.280
that you were rotten.
-No, I think

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:11.316
that I was sometimes a little --
I was holding in

00:09:11.316 --> 00:09:13.885
a kind of tantrum sometimes
at the show.

00:09:13.885 --> 00:09:15.787
-I think we all were.
-It was the sleep deprivation.

00:09:15.787 --> 00:09:18.423
It's used for torture.
-But I think

00:09:18.423 --> 00:09:21.760
that you had an ability...
-To smoke cigarette?

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:23.629
-I didn't know.
You know what I mean?

00:09:23.629 --> 00:09:25.631
Like, I knew other people
were mad at me.

00:09:25.631 --> 00:09:28.467
And that was -- Like, it was
the not knowing.

00:09:28.467 --> 00:09:29.869
-Yeah.
-Which is your own --

00:09:29.869 --> 00:09:31.971
It's a credit to your restraint,
but that's...

00:09:31.971 --> 00:09:35.540
-Yeah, but that can be weird.
Being hard to read is weird.

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:37.510
-Yeah.
-It can be a detriment.

00:09:37.510 --> 00:09:41.146
It can be good
in business negotiations.

00:09:41.146 --> 00:09:42.981
-Poker.
-Poker.

00:09:42.981 --> 00:09:45.450
Although I don't think I would
be able to hold it in in poker,

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:48.053
'cause I don't know
what's good or bad.

00:09:48.053 --> 00:09:50.021
-[ Laughs ]
-I would just be like...

00:09:50.021 --> 00:09:52.825
-[ Laughs ]
-..."jack, queen."

00:09:52.825 --> 00:09:57.196
Uh, but, yeah, my dad told me --
he said, um --

00:09:57.196 --> 00:09:59.765
he goes, "The best advice
I'll ever give you.

00:09:59.765 --> 00:10:01.533
If you're on a business call,

00:10:01.533 --> 00:10:03.902
and you don't like what
the other person is saying --

00:10:03.902 --> 00:10:05.804
let's say, you don't like
their offer

00:10:05.804 --> 00:10:07.640
or you don't like
the decision they've reached --

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:10.142
just say nothing
after they tell you.

00:10:10.142 --> 00:10:11.743
Just stay silent.

00:10:11.743 --> 00:10:13.145
After a few seconds,

00:10:13.145 --> 00:10:14.746
they'll go,
'Are you still there?'

00:10:14.746 --> 00:10:17.416
And you go, 'Uh-huh.'"
-[ Laughs ]

00:10:17.416 --> 00:10:19.651
-"And then
it'll be silent again,

00:10:19.651 --> 00:10:23.289
and then they will start to
backpedal and make concessions,

00:10:23.289 --> 00:10:25.657
because people hate silence
so much

00:10:25.657 --> 00:10:29.328
that they would rather give in
than have this continue."

00:10:29.328 --> 00:10:31.263
-That's a very effective...
-Yeah.

00:10:31.263 --> 00:10:33.431
And when he told me it,
I was an adult,

00:10:33.431 --> 00:10:36.335
and I thought, "You used that
on us when we were kids."

00:10:36.335 --> 00:10:37.636
[ Laughter ]

00:10:37.636 --> 00:10:40.106
I'd call him and be like,
"Can I see an R-rated movie?

00:10:40.106 --> 00:10:41.974
You still there?"
"Uh-huh."

00:10:41.974 --> 00:10:44.409
[ Laughter ]
"Um..."

00:10:44.409 --> 00:10:47.746
So, yes, so I would like
the speeches to include

00:10:47.746 --> 00:10:50.282
my faults, and
I would like someone --

00:10:50.282 --> 00:10:52.550
I would like
someone to point out

00:10:52.550 --> 00:10:55.787
that I really enjoyed life,
like I really liked it.

00:10:55.787 --> 00:10:57.423
And again, I'm talking
in the past tense.

00:10:57.423 --> 00:10:59.358
-Yeah.
-But I'm still here.

00:10:59.358 --> 00:11:02.261
-I'm glad you're still here.
-Thanks, man. [ Chuckles ]

00:11:02.261 --> 00:11:05.597
-And -- No, genuinely,
I'm glad you're here today.

00:11:05.597 --> 00:11:10.902
And I think the thing,
if I was at your funeral,

00:11:10.902 --> 00:11:12.872
the thing I would say --
-Yes, that's the next question.

00:11:12.872 --> 00:11:14.306
-[ Laughs ]

00:11:14.306 --> 00:11:16.108
-"If you spoke at my funeral,
what you say?"

00:11:16.108 --> 00:11:17.643
is the next question.
-Is it really?

00:11:17.643 --> 00:11:19.778
-Yeah, on my fancy list, yeah.

00:11:19.778 --> 00:11:22.581
-Um, and I said this to you
before...

00:11:22.581 --> 00:11:24.450
-You said my eulogy to me?

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:26.718
-Uh, no, it's a piece of --

00:11:26.718 --> 00:11:28.787
I always said, like, I think
one of the reasons

00:11:28.787 --> 00:11:34.160
that people are so happy
for your well-deserved success

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:37.563
is, one, that it's well-deserved
and, two, uh,

00:11:37.563 --> 00:11:40.165
all that time at "SNL,"
you were so --

00:11:40.165 --> 00:11:42.768
you were such a fan
of other people.

00:11:42.768 --> 00:11:45.303
You were so supportive
of other people.

00:11:45.303 --> 00:11:47.173
And I think it meant a lot

00:11:47.173 --> 00:11:49.474
because everyone knew,
the minute they met you,

00:11:49.474 --> 00:11:52.178
that you had sort of
impeccable comedy taste.

00:11:52.178 --> 00:11:57.048
And I think that a kind word,
that you were very gracious with

00:11:57.048 --> 00:11:58.884
and very generous with,
I think it meant a lot

00:11:58.884 --> 00:12:01.286
to a lot of people,
myself included.

00:12:01.286 --> 00:12:03.188
-I appreciate you saying that.

00:12:03.188 --> 00:12:07.059
-And certainly my life has been
a great deal richer

00:12:07.059 --> 00:12:09.294
since I met you.
-Yeah, mine, too.

00:12:09.294 --> 00:12:12.564
You know, you also
recommended me to audition,

00:12:12.564 --> 00:12:14.566
which is how I got
the writing job.

00:12:14.566 --> 00:12:17.602
And if you didn't want me
to be a writer,

00:12:17.602 --> 00:12:19.471
I wouldn't have gotten
the writing job.

00:12:19.471 --> 00:12:22.540
So you really did
change my life in...

00:12:22.540 --> 00:12:25.110
Well, I don't know.
I heard Werner Herzog say,

00:12:25.110 --> 00:12:27.079
"I don't know if people
change each other's lives,

00:12:27.079 --> 00:12:28.213
but you made my life better."

00:12:28.213 --> 00:12:30.049
'Cause it was like,

00:12:30.049 --> 00:12:32.618
I liked being a comedian.
It was great.

00:12:32.618 --> 00:12:34.953
But it was like picking up rocks
on the ground.

00:12:34.953 --> 00:12:37.356
And then you were like,
"You want to go to the moon?"

00:12:37.356 --> 00:12:38.590
-[ Laughs ]

00:12:38.590 --> 00:12:40.125
-And it's still kind of
picking up rocks,

00:12:40.125 --> 00:12:41.927
but, like, it's on the moon.

00:12:41.927 --> 00:12:44.963
No, it totally changed
the direction of my life.

00:12:44.963 --> 00:12:48.033
-Um, well, like every
conversation we have,

00:12:48.033 --> 00:12:50.936
I am happy we had it.
And I love you very much.

00:12:50.936 --> 00:12:52.037
-You don't want
the last question?

00:12:52.037 --> 00:12:53.171
-Oh, yeah,
what's the last question?

00:12:53.171 --> 00:12:55.640
-You ever seen a ghost?
-No. [ Laughs ]

00:12:55.640 --> 00:12:57.208
-Has your mom or anything?
-No.

00:12:57.208 --> 00:12:58.844
-No one?
-I thought about it

00:12:58.844 --> 00:13:02.814
when you were in "Kid Gorgeous,"
when you talk about ghosts.

00:13:02.814 --> 00:13:04.984
I thought, "I don't know.
I've never seen a ghost."

00:13:04.984 --> 00:13:09.554
-It's been my go-to icebreaker
for a while now.

00:13:09.554 --> 00:13:10.990
-Yeah.
-Normally, at, like --

00:13:10.990 --> 00:13:12.724
-By the way, a high school kid
at a party,

00:13:12.724 --> 00:13:14.627
with that outfit,
asks that question.

00:13:14.627 --> 00:13:16.261
That's what he thinks a girl
wants to hear.

00:13:16.261 --> 00:13:18.730
-Stares at her for a while.
-[ Laughs ]

00:13:18.730 --> 00:13:20.499
-"Can I help you?"
"You ever seen a ghost?"

00:13:20.499 --> 00:13:22.000
[ Laughter ]

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:23.568
Would she go out with him,
is the question.

00:13:23.568 --> 00:13:25.304
-Um...
-At the end of the movie,

00:13:25.304 --> 00:13:27.439
they'd be making out,
just for a cutaway.

00:13:27.439 --> 00:13:31.043
That's my go-to icebreaker
if I'm at a wedding

00:13:31.043 --> 00:13:33.045
and I'm sitting next to
someone I don't know --

00:13:33.045 --> 00:13:35.614
'cause I get put
at a rando table --

00:13:35.614 --> 00:13:39.251
I will often say,
"Have you ever seen a ghost?"

00:13:39.251 --> 00:13:41.920
And people, if they
have not seen a ghost...

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:46.492
And a ghost is a dead person
whose soul or spirit

00:13:46.492 --> 00:13:51.096
is now ghoulish and haunting.
-[ Chuckles ] Ghoulish.

00:13:51.096 --> 00:13:56.568
-And, normally, if they haven't,
one of their relatives has.

00:13:56.568 --> 00:14:00.138
Or like, "No, but my best
friend's house was haunted."

00:14:00.138 --> 00:14:04.209
And it's a remarkably effective
terrible icebreaker.

00:14:04.209 --> 00:14:06.344
Sometimes I use it incorrectly.

00:14:06.344 --> 00:14:08.146
Like, I'll be at a party,

00:14:08.146 --> 00:14:09.681
and I don't know how to act
at parties.

00:14:09.681 --> 00:14:11.049
And a group of people
will be talking,

00:14:11.049 --> 00:14:13.252
and I'll walk up and be like,
"How's everyone doing?

00:14:13.252 --> 00:14:15.087
You ever seen a ghost?"

00:14:15.087 --> 00:14:17.289
And then that sounds
like you are a ghost.

00:14:17.289 --> 00:14:19.458
[ Laughter ]

00:14:19.458 --> 00:14:21.460
-Oh, you mean, like,
"You have now"?

00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:23.662
-Right, "Well, you have now."
-[ Laughs ]

00:14:23.662 --> 00:14:27.232
-Then...
-[ Laughs ]

00:14:27.232 --> 00:14:29.901
-Um, yeah.

00:14:29.901 --> 00:14:32.337
-I think that's
a good place to end it.

00:14:32.337 --> 00:14:34.940
-Should we end it with
the cast of "Sneakers" again?

00:14:34.940 --> 00:14:37.576
But this time, we nail it?
[ Laughter ]

